<section id="S6.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">6.1.3.  Effect of Question Difficulty</h4>

<section id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RAG provides greater benefits on difficult questions</h5>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ RAG provides greater benefits on difficult questions ‣ 6.1.3. Effect of Question Difficulty ‣ 6.1. LLM Performance with RAG ‣ 6. Results and Discussion ‣ From Slides to Chatbots: Enhancing Large Language Models with University Course Materials" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> reports the impact of RAG when questions are grouped by difficulty levels (as defined in SciEx). RAG yields the largest gains on hard questions (+10.79%). In contrast, improvements on easy and medium questions are smaller.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T4" class="ltx_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure ltx_flex_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
